The (in)transparency of meaning change and variation
A study of the indefinite cualquiera in European and Argentinian Spanish

This repository hosts the research data and protocols accompanying the book The (in)transparency of meaning change and variation: A study of the indefinite cualquiera in European and Argentinian Spanish.

Overview

Goal. Trace the semantic development and distributional variation of the indefinite cualquiera (and historical graphical variants) across time and varieties of Spanish.

Approach. A dataset sampling from the Corpus del Español with strict philological and syntactic filtering, yielding period-balanced datasets suitable for quantitative and qualitative analysis.

Corpus and query

Primary source: Corpus del Español — Historical & Genres interface
https://www.corpusdelespanol.org/hist-gen/

Selection protocol (Methodology)

Our aim is to obtain ≥ 150 valid examples per period (i.e., examples that are philologically reliable and free of the exclusion contexts listed below).

Period sampling

For each period/century, randomly retrieve ≥ 200 contexts (seeded sampling; see “Reproducibility”).

Copy selected contexts into one spreadsheet per century (see “Repository layout”).

Philological reliability (12th–14th centuries)

For centuries 1200–1400, we exclude texts that are not philologically reliable based on Octavio & Molina (2017) (“Cordemáforo”). Each text is assigned one of:

V — very reliable: always included

A — reliable: included if V-texts are insufficient

R — not reliable: excluded

N — not found in list: excluded

The file docs/cordemaforo_list.md (or .csv) in this repo contains the full list and decisions used.

Syntactic exclusions (all periods)

Exclude any context where cualquiera (or a variant) appears in:

coordination (e.g., X y cualquiera / cualquiera o Z),

verbal ellipsis, or

comparison clauses.

If the period’s valid count is still < 150

Sample more randomized contexts and re-apply the filters.

If still insufficient:

1200–1400 only:
a) take all contexts from V/A texts and apply syntactic filters;
b) if still short, include remaining occurrences from V/A shown by the corpus (after filtering).

Other periods: sample additional randomized contexts and re-apply syntactic filters.

1200–1400 only (last resort): selectively classify some N/R items until reaching ≥ 150 valid cases.

What counts as a “valid case”?

A line in which a token matching *ualqu* is:

from an eligible text (per the rules above), and

not in coordination, verbal ellipsis, or comparison, and

judged philologically reliable (centuries 1200–1400), and

assigned a variant label (cualquier, cualquiera, qualquier, qualquiera, qualquiere).

Each case includes left and right context to support interpretation.

